From 3a1f6735f0585c7cb3a228e9991aad34c8393d5c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 14:43:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] digitizer: a satin arm starts where its own ribbon does,
 not at the junction blob's radius

A junction-anchored stroke stepped back by `field.half_at(node)` before its
first cross. That is exactly right on a clean T -- the node's distance
transform IS the crossing bar's half-width, so the step lands the first cross
on the bar's edge -- and wrong on a real letterform, where `half_at` is an
OMNIDIRECTIONAL clearance and the widest thing near a bold corner is a
diagonal across the whole blob. Measured on `becker_lc_large`'s "E", whose
stroke half-width is 1.75mm: the top-bar arm reads 2.67 at its node while its
corridor is already flat at 2.33 by 1.25mm in, so the fixed rule ate a full
millimetre of ribbon that was never part of the junction.

`_junction_entry_mm` walks the corridor in until it stops narrowing and starts
the ribbon there, held between two bounds -- never more than the old offset
(so this can only give ribbon back, never take a millimetre that sews today)
and never less than the shape's own half-width (whatever arm this one meets is
at least that wide and has to be cleared; without the floor both arms of a
corner recover past each other and sew it TWICE, measured on that same "E").
The floor is also what makes the rule bit-identical on T_SHAPE and
T_SHORT_STEM, where the two bounds coincide.

The walk's None answer is the other half, and it is the wedge. Thinning a bold
corner forks the skeleton diagonally into it and leaves a 3-4mm twig whose
corridor ramps from the blob straight to a point with no flat stretch anywhere
along it -- 2.33mm down to 0.17mm at a near-constant 0.66mm/mm on the same "E".
There is no ribbon in a twig to find the start of: a zigzag across it is the
radiating fan. Those are left un-sewn for the arms they grew out of, guarded by
a length bound so a genuinely tapering LEG keeps its column -- the "R"'s leg
measures 3.6 half-widths against the twigs' 1.5-2.1, and T_SHORT_STEM's stem
4.0 (with a plateau anyway, so the guard never has to speak for it).

Measured: corpus mean 70.62 -> 70.92, 19 designs up, 3 down (all <=0.2), 1
flat. 4.6% of skeleton spine length stops being sewn across the five proof
designs; no shape is newly left un-sewn on any design checked. Full suite
unchanged at its 7 pre-existing failures (5 tesseract, 2 platform goldens),
1135 passed.

NOT fixed here, recorded because the brief expected it to be: the large
radiating pattern in hairline stroke renders of BECKER's letters is not these
twigs. It is each letter's own 25-30mm main column, which stabilizes at its
junction end perfectly well, and at real thread width (0.5mm) those shapes
already render 87-95% solid -- the fan is mostly an artifact of drawing
stitches as hairlines. Those designs lose their scorecard points to `direction`
(0.60) and `sttype` (0.55), not coverage (0.91): the pro FILLS letters we
satin.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Ek9Z1DnykHo5mSfUdgyzxc
