From 070a1136dd58931af6b493f47c05ed29b5d1ac7b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 16:07:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] digitizer(satin): never build a column on a corner fork
 (lane B)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The radiating fan at a letterform junction has (at least) two families, and
this fixes one of them outright: the CORNER FORK.

Thinning a bold corner does not stop at the corner. The medial axis forks
and runs a branch into the corner POINT, where the distance transform is
zero. `_prune_spurs` erases the short ones; a bold stroke's corner reaches
about 1.4 half-widths past the node, so the rest survive as full skeleton
edges — and then two bad things happen. They become strokes of their own,
anchored in the junction blob at one end and pinched to nothing at the
other: a WEDGE, and a zigzag across a wedge IS the starburst. And they are
the best weld candidate any bar's end has (a fork's tangent is the most
anti-aligned thing at that node), so a real bar welds to one and its column
hooks 45 deg into the cap corner and sprays there — measured on
`becker_lc_large`'s "E" on both its top and bottom bar, and on its "A",
whose whole apex was one 12 mm sunburst.

  * `_corner_forks` identifies them from geometry alone: exactly one free
    end whose corridor is essentially zero (a point, not a cap), no longer
    than 1.7x the corridor at its node (past that the vertex is ACUTE and
    the fork is the only skeleton the letter's point has — `mfab_lc`'s "W"
    went bare at 1.78), and narrowing at every probe from node to tip (a
    plateau is a ribbon, and a ribbon is a stroke — the "R"'s leg holds
    2.17 mm flat for 3 mm before it tapers).
  * They are removed BEFORE welding, so nothing can hook onto one.
  * A node left with fewer than three arms is then not a junction at all.
    One arm — the wider corridor — is re-flagged FREE and runs out to the
    artwork edge, covering the corner square with its own column; the other
    tucks under it, clearing that arm's MEASURED corridor instead of the
    omnidirectional blob reading. Without that half, deleting a fork would
    just leave the corner bare.

Also here, both scoped to those corner ends so every byte-identical golden
still holds: the cap extension aims itself over one stroke width instead of
one raster segment, and stops 0.03 mm short of the edge so the terminal
cross keeps both rails.

Measured over the five proof designs (own output dir, same scorecard):

  scores      72.9->72.4  69.7->69.4  57.7->57.4  71.5->72.0  71.6->71.3
  cross swing 6038->5029  5911->4879  7875->7305  7204->6443  5634->4870
              (total degrees of cross rotation, the fan's own signature)
  bare fabric 8.9%->8.5%  9.2%->8.2%  -           4.6%->4.8%  10.4%->9.6%

The scorecard is flat-to-slightly-down; the fan is visibly gone from the
corner-fork cases and the letters cover MORE fabric than before. What it
does not touch: 3+-arm junction blobs (the "A"'s counter, the "R"'s bowl)
and acute vertices, which still fan.

One new test failure, reported not papered over:
`test_chaining_cuts_the_benchmark_fixtures_trim_rate` reads 4.55 trims/1k
against a 4.1 ceiling (baseline 3.4) — 3 extra trims on enthusiast_logo,
because columns that end at the artwork edge instead of tucked in a blob
give stage 7 fewer short hops to link. `test_a_stem_crossing_three_
junctions_welds_into_one_stroke` keeps every assertion it ever made and
picks the stem by the one thing that is true of a stem (it runs the glyph's
height) instead of by longest spine, which stopped being the stem the
moment its two corner forks were no longer welded into it.

